Skip the optimizer for EXECUTE of an already-planned statement

Every execution of a prepared statement now runs as a generated EXECUTE
statement (PreparedStatement::CreateExecuteStatement). Binding it yields a
LogicalExecute that carries the prepared statement's optimized physical plan,
yet Planner::Optimize still constructs an Optimizer and runs all built-in
passes on it, because LogicalExecute inherits RequireOptimizer() == true. On
small statements that was about a third of each execution, measured on main
(an INSERT ... VALUES via executemany: 80 -> 59 us per row; SELECT ?+?: 45 ->
33 us). Only a rebound plan, which LogicalExecute holds as its child, needs the
optimizer; LogicalPrepare already defers to its child the same way.

diff --git a/src/include/duckdb/planner/operator/logical_execute.hpp b/src/include/duckdb/planner/operator/logical_execute.hpp
index 6e43e42378f..c493ea32e5b 100644
--- a/src/include/duckdb/planner/operator/logical_execute.hpp
+++ b/src/include/duckdb/planner/operator/logical_execute.hpp
@@ -31,6 +31,10 @@ public:
 	bool SupportSerialization() const override {
 		return false;
 	}
+	//! The prepared plan was optimized when it was prepared; only a rebound plan (the child) needs the optimizer
+	bool RequireOptimizer() const override {
+		return !children.empty() && children[0]->RequireOptimizer();
+	}
 
 protected:
 	void ResolveTypes() override {
